#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:4
#: 5b401d0029ac41c5b5059c69cf4cf7e1
msgid "IMU Temperature Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:6
#: fccf63abf9524d1da5b2b166b7edd836
msgid "ArduPilot supports IMU temperature calibration. Calibrating the IMU for temperature changes can greatly reduce the amount of variation in the IMU and helps with flying in conditions where the temperature varies a lot between boot-up and flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:8
#: 2b66d7d7db7b4c34abe766fa74574a8f
msgid "this feature is only available currently on 2MB autopilots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:10
#: 594b9453c15b4e77a752045597b40212
msgid "Temperature calibration is done by first setting up some parameters to establish the start of a calibration routine on the next boot-up, then taking the autopilot and placing it in a cold environment for several minutes to establish a lower calibration temperature. Then, quickly re-powering (and, optionally connecting to a GCS to monitor the process) in an environment at the hotter, upper calibration point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:12
#: 355a8817dc1a4dbd8a4539504c050f0d
msgid "You must not allow the autopilot to move, once re-powered, until the calibration is completed!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:14
#: 346a82e22ea84b1fbd00446378994260
msgid "After the calibration is complete, you can also run an Offline calibration tool, to verify the calibration improvement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:16
#: 1aa0e5dbd23e47f9bd1add656eed4d3b
msgid "The temperature calibration is stored in ArduPilot parameters, just like other types of calibration parameters. There is also the option to store the parameters in a way that persists across a full parameter reset, which allows support for factory calibration for vendors selling ArduPilot compatible autopilots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:23
#: 4858d490e3cd4f559a0da91b07218762
msgid "Temperature Calibration Procedure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:25
#: 84ab56f0a2b74a52824a1918b881aeb2
msgid "The key factors to a good temperature calibration are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:27
#: b93a6ba5483d4c49a8bded8c1d32b08a
msgid "letting the autopilot cool to a temperature below the minimum operating temperature before starting the calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:30
#: 2672b9eed30441c59b4249d6be99e8c8
msgid "ensuring the IMU temperature that is reached during the calibration run is high enough to cover all flight conditions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:33
#: 8a3ead87066c4c5382782d1c97976a81
msgid "ensuring that there is no movement during the calibration. Note that the autopilot does not need to be level, but it must not move."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:37
#: 3113105a43b44e1898baea3e76421790
msgid "Before you start the calibration process you need to setup some key parameters:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:40
#: 977e46f79ab04130a63553fbdb325b4d
msgid "make sure the :ref:`INS_ACCn_CALTEMP <INS_ACC1_CALTEMP>` and :ref:`INS_GYRn_CALTEMP <INS_GYR1_CALTEMP>` parameters are already set (there is one parameter per IMU, make sure you set all of them). These are set automatically when you calibrate the accelerometers and gyroscopes. They record the temperature of the IMU at the time the offsets were calibrated, which is key information for the calibration process. If they are not already set (they default to -300 which is an invalid value) then you need to perform a 6-axis accel calibration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:50
#: 4d89c985e6164494919812f43b4168b6
msgid "set :ref:`INS_TCALn_ENABLE <INS_TCAL1_ENABLE>` to 2 for each IMU to enable IMU temperature calibration learning on the each IMU you want to calibrate. A value of 2 enables temperature calibration learning. The ENABLE parameter value of 2 takes effect on the next boot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:56
#: 9b2a1467599d4b53b5088da47bd37de1
msgid "set :ref:`INS_TCALn_TMAX <INS_TCAL1_TMAX>` to the temperature in degrees centigrade that you want to stop the calibration. This should be the high temperature that you expect to reach in your calibration run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:61
#: 75051f26470548ce963c896c94e23c70
msgid "it is recommended that you ensure a microSD card is inserted and you have :ref:`LOG_DISARMED <LOG_DISARMED>` set to 1 to log the IMU data used in the calibration process. While this is only required for the offline calibration check below, using it for the initial calibration is recommended in case you wish to inspect the raw data and to check for any movement that may have disturbed the calibration process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:68
#: 1ca1de004c534b3387c2ff1f3f7f6b1e
msgid "For boards with a builtin IMU heater you may wish to raise :ref:`BRD_HEAT_TARG<BRD_HEAT_TARG>` to a higher than usual temperature. A value of 70 can be good for calibration. Make sure you change it back to a normal flight value (normally 45) when the calibration process is complete. A value is 70 is likely to be above what can be reached, so for boards with heaters a reasonable value for TMAX is 65 degrees C. Note that on some boards (notably the CubePilot CubeBlack and CubeOrange) the 3rd IMU is not close to the heater, so it's maximum temperature needs to be about 10 degrees below the first two IMUs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:79
#: 71a8a10b429b4076a979d09504a9dfa6
msgid "Once the parameters are setup, and the autopilot cooled to the minimum temperature, you should power on the flight controller in a warm environment, ensuring that it will not move throughout the calibration process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:83
#: f56bb4f17aea4b6b98219a34c51794cd
msgid "The calibration process has a 10 minute timeout. If an IMU doesn't increase it's temperature by at least 0.5 degrees C over a 10 minute period then the calibration will complete and the parameters will be saved up to the point the temperature reached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:88
#: 784b9c6352d0409cb970f659c80305d3
msgid "The parameters are also saved for intermediate temperatures throughout the calibration process. So if you run out of time (or patience) waiting for the calibration then you can stop it at any time by powering off the board. If you do this then you will need to manually change the :ref:`INS_TCALn_ENABLE <INS_TCAL1_ENABLE>` parameters from 2 to 1 to enable the new calibration data to be used. Otherwise it will start the calibration again on each boot. If you do interrupt the calibration process then you should power off the vehicle with a minimum of movement or you could invalidate the calibration. Stopping the calibration process by using a telemetry link to change the :ref:`INS_TCALn_ENABLE <INS_TCAL1_ENABLE>` parameters to 1 before powering off can also be done and is a good idea."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:101
#: 9e57c1f68e44488da10a9a6d7bb4c111
msgid "Note that the calibration process requires a minimum of 10 degrees C change in temperature between the starting and ending temperature. A range of at least 25 degrees C is recommended, ie from the kitchen freezer to your desk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:106
#: d9003782a932468388643adb5a181c33
msgid "Monitoring the Calibration Process"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:108
#: 99c1089560504805b0fc6e144124e891
msgid "If you have a buzzer connected then you will here a short periodic beep while the calibration is in progress. When the calibration is complete a completion tune will play. If you have a mavlink telemetry link to the vehicle then you can also monitor the progress via that link, graphing the IMU temperatures (which are in the RAW_IMU, SCALED_IMU2 and SCALED_IMU3 messages if you have mavlink2 enabled)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:115
#: c77e84bbd9cd46d5b3382168d9485be0
msgid "On completion the :ref:`INS_TCALn_ENABLE <INS_TCAL1_ENABLE>` parameters will change to 1 for \"enable\" for each IMU. Once all your IMUs have completed calibration you can reboot the vehicle. You should also:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:120
#: 6c8185a627ed4377b74bb3fec0f86b8e
msgid "reset the :ref:`BRD_HEAT_TARG<BRD_HEAT_TARG>` back to its normal value (usually 45) if you changed it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:123
#: 48eb3325517d4c90904c55c6ac9706a6
msgid "disable :ref:`LOG_DISARMED <LOG_DISARMED>` if you had enabled it and want it disabled for normal usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:126
#: bae9bd8a461f4ecbbaa1fe5d884691f1
msgid "You may also wish to refresh your parameters and save all the parameters that start with the INS_TCAL prefix. You can restore these parameters to avoid needing a new temperature calibration if you ever do a complete reset of your parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:132
#: da67650a625043a58f43dc583876b5fa
msgid "Offline Calibration Tool"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:134
#: 8749c7b135c040c7874c63b0bb6a644c
msgid "The procedure outlined above will have completed an automatic online temperature calibration. If you had :ref:`LOG_DISARMED <LOG_DISARMED>` set to 1 then the onboard log file allows you to do an offline calibration check. Doing an offline calibration has the advantage that you can inspect the improvements to the calibration by graphing the uncorrected and corrected calibration for the accelerometers and gyroscopes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:142
#: bdf8c44f2837421f953133a274c0b297
msgid "To perform an offline calibration you need to use the script in the ArduPilot git repository in Tools/scripts/tempcal_IMU.py. When you run this script on a log file from a calibration you will get two sets of graphs. One set is for the gyroscopes the other is for the accelerometers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:147
#: 0a3c1b596c2d4806ad1e13281b13f31e
msgid "The image below shows the results of a run on a log file for 3 IMU board:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:155
#: 0240a360cf9547ca83bfa8c18d4ea4fc
msgid "The key features of the graphs are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:157
#: a64dacdaa1a848a18998ccf12e65c370
msgid "the first 3 lines on each graph (blue, orange and green) show the uncorrected IMU data. This allows you to see how much drift you have without temperature correction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:161
#: 098ac6216d3743688c85bd8ecb21dcfb
msgid "the next 3 graphs show the corrected IMU data, showing how much the temperature calibration will help. For a good calibration these lines will all be close to zero"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:165
#: 5eb5313a35f6487f80e02e0958486808
msgid "the IMU temperature is shown on the right hand scale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:167
#: a00f62f75be44bb3925ff00d30c82077
msgid "you should check for any sudden changes in the data, which indicates that the board was not kept still when calibrating"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:170
#: c83bc7f245a3454a849fc94e48efb9c3
msgid "with multiple IMUs the graphs may span different temperature ranges and times. This happens as the IMUs reach their target calibration temperatures at different times"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:174
#: 15a61ef3009d4e8a97472e63bc29a1fc
msgid "The calibration script will also save a copy of the new calibration parameters to the file tcal.parm, or a filename chosen with the --outfile command line option. You should load these parameters to enable the new calibration on the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:179
#: e43d2a95786b443da6cfb2551cf92711
msgid "Other command line options are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:181
#: 723f8fb6deb645238a398ca2367e5c5f
msgid "the --no-graph option disables the graphing, allowing for batch processing of log files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:184
#: cb6a5386e264469c9cb0ea528729263e
msgid "the --online option changes the calibration process to use the same algorithm as the online calibration method used inside the flight controller. This is useful for debugging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:188
#: cd2aa1149a3d45cf9d2c80842325f781
msgid "the --tclr option enables the use of the TCLR messages from the log instead of the IMU log messages. The TCLR messages record the exact filtered data used in the online calibration process. This is useful for debugging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:193
#: 3030b27e52124988b9498cd91e61a367
msgid "the --log-parm option enables an additional set of graphs showing the IMU data with corrections applied using existing INS_TCAL parameters from your log file. This allows you to compare the new calibration against a previous temperature calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:199
#: 11f2ed8d438148539063b37dad3ca65c
msgid "Factory Temperature Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:201
#: cb80a375f0e647d39f450dc2bf1fdadc
msgid "On autopilots based on the STM32F7 and STM32H7 microcontrollers we support storing temperature calibration parameters in persistent storage in the boards bootloader sector. This allows the calibration parameters to persist even with a complete parameter reset, including changing firmware type. This allows a vendor to ship autopilots pre-calibrated from the factory which is a great convenience for users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imutempcal.rst:209
#: 72c40d6f09fe43a78b6fb870860982b0
msgid "To save parameters persistently, you should set the desired bits in :ref:`INS_TCAL_OPTIONS <INS_TCAL_OPTIONS>` to indicate persistence of Temperature Calibration and Accel Calibrations. As a final step send a \"flash bootloader\" mavlink command to the autopilot. This will re-flash the bootloader with calibration parameters embedded in the final part of the bootloader sector in flash. You can inspect these parameters using the @SYS/persistent.parm sysfs file using a mavftp client."
msgstr ""
